      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Toronto Blue Jays' season is a study in frustrating whiplash, capped by a <strong>humiliating 3-0 shutout loss to the White Sox Sunday</strong> that exposed an offense unable to back rookie pitcher Jimmy Yesavage. At 46-53 and sitting 10½ games behind in the AL East, this team is a ghost of its early contender hopes, now clinging to a one-game losing streak after a bizarre three-game split in Chicago that included a 12-4 blowout and that lone 1-0 grit win. Yesavage looked sharp, but the bats went silent - again - as the Jays scored just one run across their last two games against a struggling White Sox rotation. The rotation is holding, but without run support, even brilliance evaporates. <strong>Tonight at 7:07 PM ET, they face the division-leading Rays (46-53) in a must-win to stop the slide and prove they're still alive in a collapsing race.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 20, 2026"
